00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:05.350
So what makes BigQuery special?

00:02:05.350 --> 00:02:07.220
It starts with the
true separation

00:02:07.220 --> 00:02:10.400
of storage and compute, which
allows you to store petabytes

00:02:10.400 --> 00:02:12.650
of data in storage,
but pay pennies

00:02:12.650 --> 00:02:14.630
per day for your queries.

00:02:14.630 --> 00:02:17.900
Data is stored in Colossus,
which is BigQuery's columnar

00:02:17.900 --> 00:02:21.800
storage, which is encrypted,
replicated, and distributed,

00:02:21.800 --> 00:02:25.340
making it highly durable
against failures.

00:02:25.340 --> 00:02:27.110
When you send your
queries to BigQuery,

00:02:27.110 --> 00:02:30.650
they run in stages over a
massive farm of compute units

00:02:30.650 --> 00:02:34.760
called slots, which are units
of computational capacity.

00:02:34.760 --> 00:02:37.010
BigQuery automatically
calculates how many

00:02:37.010 --> 00:02:39.320
stages are needed
for every query

00:02:39.320 --> 00:02:43.450
and dynamically calculate the
slots needed for each stage.

00:02:43.450 --> 00:02:46.750
Data moves from the
storage to the query server

00:02:46.750 --> 00:02:49.570
at lightning speed over
the petabit scale network

00:02:49.570 --> 00:02:51.520
called Jupiter network.

00:02:51.520 --> 00:02:53.920
Even complex queries,
which need to store

00:02:53.920 --> 00:02:57.040
data between the query stages,
have a persistent store

00:02:57.040 --> 00:03:00.700
called shuffle, which
is entirely in memory.

00:03:00.700 --> 00:03:02.920
All of these put
together is what

00:03:02.920 --> 00:03:07.695
makes BigQuery deliver blazing
fast performance to you.

00:03:07.695 --> 00:03:09.320
Now we'll take a look
at a sample query

00:03:09.320 --> 00:03:12.960
to see how it works within
the BigQuery architecture.

00:03:12.960 --> 00:03:14.780
This is an example
of a scan filter

00:03:14.780 --> 00:03:18.230
aggregate query on a table
containing one billion page

00:03:18.230 --> 00:03:21.930
view logs from Wikipedia where
the title contains Google.

00:03:21.930 --> 00:03:25.040
The queries are first routed
to a query master, which

00:03:25.040 --> 00:03:27.440
contacts the metadata
server to figure out

00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:29.990
exactly where the
physical data resides

00:03:29.990 --> 00:03:32.960
and how it's partitioned to come
up with the initial execution

00:03:32.960 --> 00:03:34.340
plan.

00:03:34.340 --> 00:03:36.410
The first stage of this
query sends the query

00:03:36.410 --> 00:03:39.050
to a lot of parallel
workers, each of which

00:03:39.050 --> 00:03:41.630
reads the title
column and filters out

00:03:41.630 --> 00:03:43.930
the data that don't match.

00:03:43.930 --> 00:03:45.650
It then does a
partial aggregation

00:03:45.650 --> 00:03:48.350
as directed by the GROUP
BY, and writes the results

00:03:48.350 --> 00:03:51.330
into shuffle using
a hash of the title.

00:03:51.330 --> 00:03:53.250
The next stage reads
from the shuffled output

00:03:53.250 --> 00:03:56.490
from the previous stage and
does the final aggregation.

00:03:56.490 --> 00:03:59.650
The final stage, or stage
3, is extremely simple.

00:03:59.650 --> 00:04:01.320
It just reads the
resulting titles

00:04:01.320 --> 00:04:04.850
and sorts them in an
alphabetical descending order.

00:04:04.850 --> 00:04:08.180
This final sort operation
is done on a single worker

00:04:08.180 --> 00:04:11.710
for serving up the results
to the [INAUDIBLE]..

00:04:11.710 --> 00:04:15.182
Now that we know how
BigQuery runs queries,

00:04:15.182 --> 00:04:17.140
let's look at some of
the best practices on how

00:04:17.140 --> 00:04:20.350
to run these queries fast and
efficient within BigQuery.

00:04:23.260 --> 00:04:24.860
A typical query
consists of a select,

00:04:24.860 --> 00:04:29.410
from, where, group by,
and order by clauses.

00:04:29.410 --> 00:04:31.410
Since BigQuery is
a columnar store,

00:04:31.410 --> 00:04:34.651
we're going to start out
with how to optimize select.

00:04:34.651 --> 00:04:37.250
Select star may actually
drive up your costs

00:04:37.250 --> 00:04:40.130
if you have a really wide
table with lots of columns.

00:04:40.130 --> 00:04:41.770
So the first tip
I have for you is

00:04:41.770 --> 00:04:44.560
to use only the necessary
columns in your select clause,

00:04:44.560 --> 00:04:45.520
instead of select star.

00:04:48.080 --> 00:04:50.450
Next, when using
functions, consider

00:04:50.450 --> 00:04:53.840
using approximate functions,
such as approx_count_distinct

00:04:53.840 --> 00:04:55.625
or approximate
quantiles, instead

00:04:55.625 --> 00:04:57.470
of the corresponding
exact functions,

00:04:57.470 --> 00:05:00.120
such as count_distinct
or quantiles.

00:05:00.120 --> 00:05:02.330
These are approximate
functions produce results

00:05:02.330 --> 00:05:05.570
which are 99% accurate if
you're looking for trend data

00:05:05.570 --> 00:05:09.650
and not [? to import ?] exact
values, such as financial data.

00:05:09.650 --> 00:05:11.570
Now, we'll come
to where the where

00:05:11.570 --> 00:05:14.660
clauses, where you can drive
a lot of query optimization

00:05:14.660 --> 00:05:16.190
and efficiency.

00:05:16.190 --> 00:05:17.750
The key to improving
efficiency is

00:05:17.750 --> 00:05:20.210
to filter early and
use predicates, which

00:05:20.210 --> 00:05:23.250
can be pushed down into the
tables to filter out data,

00:05:23.250 --> 00:05:25.520
especially when joining
multiple tables.

00:05:25.520 --> 00:05:29.570
Use the column directly to
match values is a lot more

00:05:29.570 --> 00:05:32.830
efficient than applying a
function on a column master

00:05:32.830 --> 00:05:34.940
value, since this predicate
can be pushed down.

00:05:38.590 --> 00:05:42.430
Next, we'll come to joins in
the where clause, any complex

00:05:42.430 --> 00:05:44.970
query involving joining
one or more tables

00:05:44.970 --> 00:05:47.020
and finding matching
relationships

00:05:47.020 --> 00:05:48.970
through the JOIN columns.

00:05:48.970 --> 00:05:51.340
There are different types of
joins, such as inner join,

00:05:51.340 --> 00:05:54.980
cross join, left and right
join, which are well-known.

00:05:54.980 --> 00:05:56.750
Speaking of how
they're executed,

00:05:56.750 --> 00:05:59.770
joins are executed in
BigQuery in several ways,

00:05:59.770 --> 00:06:03.320
a broadcast join is where
a large table on the left

00:06:03.320 --> 00:06:06.350
is joined with a small table
on the right of the join.

00:06:06.350 --> 00:06:09.560
In BigQuery, a broadcast
join takes the small table

00:06:09.560 --> 00:06:12.470
and sends the entire table
to every worker working

00:06:12.470 --> 00:06:14.450
on the large table
column values,

00:06:14.450 --> 00:06:17.630
if the table on the right
is sufficiently small.

00:06:17.630 --> 00:06:19.120
To achieve this
efficiency, you may

00:06:19.120 --> 00:06:21.920
need to filter and aggregate
the data from the small table

00:06:21.920 --> 00:06:24.620
before joining with
the large tables.

00:06:24.620 --> 00:06:26.980
The other type of join
executor within BigQuery

00:06:26.980 --> 00:06:30.250
is a hash join, where
you're joining large tables.

00:06:30.250 --> 00:06:33.640
Now, this is computationally
more intensive.

00:06:33.640 --> 00:06:35.950
The goal of the hash
join optimization

00:06:35.950 --> 00:06:38.110
is to ensure balanced
data distribution

00:06:38.110 --> 00:06:39.910
across the join tables.

00:06:39.910 --> 00:06:42.360
So keep an eye
out for data skew.

00:06:42.360 --> 00:06:44.860
To give an example of
data skew, the number

00:06:44.860 --> 00:06:48.190
of cricket players per capita
is skewed to the 12 member

00:06:48.190 --> 00:06:49.780
countries of the
International Cricket

00:06:49.780 --> 00:06:53.140
Council versus the countries
in the rest of the world.

00:06:53.140 --> 00:06:55.900
When this happens, you may
need to query the skewed data

00:06:55.900 --> 00:06:58.060
separately from the
other data and combine

00:06:58.060 --> 00:07:00.100
the results using
union, to maximize

00:07:00.100 --> 00:07:02.290
the efficiency of the query.

00:07:02.290 --> 00:07:05.210
A special case of
join is self join,

00:07:05.210 --> 00:07:08.160
when a data is
joined with itself.

00:07:08.160 --> 00:07:10.690
This type of query can
get very expensive.

00:07:10.690 --> 00:07:14.850
Some of the techniques you can
use to get the data efficiently

00:07:14.850 --> 00:07:16.840
is to first take one pass at it.

00:07:16.840 --> 00:07:19.590
So for example, if you
have a table containing

00:07:19.590 --> 00:07:22.560
male and female baby names,
you can use an if function

00:07:22.560 --> 00:07:25.920
to create pseudo columns and
compute both sets of aggregates

00:07:25.920 --> 00:07:27.900
in one pass.

00:07:27.900 --> 00:07:30.900
Another option if you have
multiple records with dependent

00:07:30.900 --> 00:07:35.250
data-- for example, a start
and end time and date for bike

00:07:35.250 --> 00:07:35.970
rides--

00:07:35.970 --> 00:07:38.610
you can use the lead
and lag functions

00:07:38.610 --> 00:07:41.070
to specify a window within
which you [INAUDIBLE]

00:07:41.070 --> 00:07:42.540
to find the matching entry.

00:07:46.500 --> 00:07:50.200
To understand how
these queries work,

00:07:50.200 --> 00:07:53.400
it is important to look at
the actual execution plan.

00:07:53.400 --> 00:07:55.620
The execution plan
for the query is

00:07:55.620 --> 00:07:58.740
available under the execution
details for every query

00:07:58.740 --> 00:08:00.810
in the BigQuery user interface.

00:08:00.810 --> 00:08:02.640
It shows you the
stages that the query

00:08:02.640 --> 00:08:05.610
took and provides the
details of the operations

00:08:05.610 --> 00:08:07.860
and timings of each stage.

00:08:07.860 --> 00:08:10.650
Look for the waits, which
indicates the stage that

00:08:10.650 --> 00:08:12.880
is waiting for a dependent
stage to complete,

00:08:12.880 --> 00:08:15.920
which can often be
a source of delay.

00:08:15.920 --> 00:08:19.430
Also look for variances between
the average and maximum worker

00:08:19.430 --> 00:08:20.205
timings.

00:08:20.205 --> 00:08:21.705
This indicates that
the distribution

00:08:21.705 --> 00:08:25.760
of work across the workers
may not be uniform.

00:08:25.760 --> 00:08:28.130
Now that you've identified
query inefficiencies,

00:08:28.130 --> 00:08:30.500
what can you do to improve them?

00:08:30.500 --> 00:08:33.679
BigQuery provides several
data organization techniques,

00:08:33.679 --> 00:08:35.630
such as partitioning
and clustering,

00:08:35.630 --> 00:08:38.690
to speed up your query
and lower the cost.

00:08:38.690 --> 00:08:40.280
Partitioning in
BigQuery allows you

00:08:40.280 --> 00:08:43.429
to divide large tables into
smaller logical tables called

00:08:43.429 --> 00:08:44.960
partitions.

00:08:44.960 --> 00:08:47.270
Data for one partition
is stored in a physically

00:08:47.270 --> 00:08:49.610
separate location from
the other partitions,

00:08:49.610 --> 00:08:51.950
each with its own metadata.

00:08:51.950 --> 00:08:53.750
When a query is
issued representing

00:08:53.750 --> 00:08:56.540
a specific partition
value, BigQuery only

00:08:56.540 --> 00:09:00.250
scans those logical tables
matching the partition value,

00:09:00.250 --> 00:09:02.360
instead of the entire table.

00:09:02.360 --> 00:09:05.090
Partitions are usually designed
for low cardinality fields,

00:09:05.090 --> 00:09:08.000
such as fields with a low number
of distinct values-- generally

00:09:08.000 --> 00:09:10.930
of the order of a few thousand.

00:09:10.930 --> 00:09:15.640
Clustering is a different
form of storage optimization

00:09:15.640 --> 00:09:19.540
within BigQuery that stores the
data in a semi-sorted format

00:09:19.540 --> 00:09:21.100
based on the cluster
key, which make

00:09:21.100 --> 00:09:23.237
consistent one or more columns.

00:09:23.237 --> 00:09:25.570
You can use a combination of
partitioning and clustering

00:09:25.570 --> 00:09:27.910
to optimize your
query by identifying

00:09:27.910 --> 00:09:30.500
the low cardinality and
high cardinality columns

00:09:30.500 --> 00:09:33.838
as candidates for
partitioning and clustering.

00:09:33.838 --> 00:09:35.755
We recently announced
support for partitioning

00:09:35.755 --> 00:09:39.220
of non-partitioned tables,
so you can speed up

00:09:39.220 --> 00:09:42.010
key look-ups for even
high-cardinality data which

00:09:42.010 --> 00:09:45.680
are not partitioned,
such as user IDs.

00:09:45.680 --> 00:09:47.540
In addition to
looking up queries,

00:09:47.540 --> 00:09:51.740
you can also improve them cost
efficiency of DML operations

00:09:51.740 --> 00:09:54.740
through partition pruning, by
using the underscore partition

00:09:54.740 --> 00:09:58.370
time pseudo column for
ingested time partition tables

00:09:58.370 --> 00:10:01.760
or by using the table
timestamp column for partition

00:10:01.760 --> 00:10:03.747
tables in your DML statement.

00:10:06.490 --> 00:10:08.160
The final recommendation
for query speed

00:10:08.160 --> 00:10:12.340
up is the use of Materialized
Views and BI Engine.

00:10:12.340 --> 00:10:14.530
Materialized Views
are pre-computed views

00:10:14.530 --> 00:10:17.940
at cache results of a query
for increased performance

00:10:17.940 --> 00:10:19.200
and efficiency.

00:10:19.200 --> 00:10:22.140
BigQuery leverages these
pre-computed results

00:10:22.140 --> 00:10:26.130
from Materialized Views
to deliver the results.

00:10:26.130 --> 00:10:29.460
If the data changes, BigQuery
only reads the delta changes

00:10:29.460 --> 00:10:32.730
from the base table to compute
the latest results, which

00:10:32.730 --> 00:10:34.460
is much more efficient.

00:10:34.460 --> 00:10:38.190
BigQuery is also smart enough to
detect when a query can benefit

00:10:38.190 --> 00:10:40.920
from a Materialized View
and automatically redirects

00:10:40.920 --> 00:10:43.710
the query to the cached results
for a Materialized View,

00:10:43.710 --> 00:10:46.950
instead of querying the
base table from storage.

00:10:46.950 --> 00:10:50.430
BI Engine takes the speed
improvements up another level

00:10:50.430 --> 00:10:53.120
by providing an in-memory
store for BigQuery.

00:10:53.120 --> 00:10:56.360
To enable this, just reserve
the BI Engine memory capacity

00:10:56.360 --> 00:10:59.410
in the same location as your
data set, and that's it.

00:10:59.410 --> 00:11:02.000
Your queries will now run
fast and always fresh.

00:11:02.000 --> 00:11:04.170
To store and manage
data, you need

00:11:04.170 --> 00:11:06.560
to bring data into BigQuery.

00:11:06.560 --> 00:11:09.050
BigQuery supports batch
and streaming methods

00:11:09.050 --> 00:11:10.460
of data ingest.

00:11:10.460 --> 00:11:12.560
Data can be loaded into
BigQuery from a variety

00:11:12.560 --> 00:11:16.340
of storage formats,
ranging from Avro to JSON.

00:11:16.340 --> 00:11:19.250
You can even use DML to
perform ELT to transform

00:11:19.250 --> 00:11:22.340
data using BigQuery API.

00:11:22.340 --> 00:11:24.440
We've removed the limits
of the number of DMLs

00:11:24.440 --> 00:11:27.440
a table can process
per day, which

00:11:27.440 --> 00:11:32.150
is much more beneficial to you
now and is much more useful.

00:11:32.150 --> 00:11:34.370
If you need to perform
continuous ingest,

00:11:34.370 --> 00:11:37.010
you'll need to use the streaming
API to get more real time

00:11:37.010 --> 00:11:38.600
information.

00:11:38.600 --> 00:11:41.330
If you need really accurate data
that is still in the streaming

00:11:41.330 --> 00:11:44.720
buffer or partition tables, you
can use an additional clause

00:11:44.720 --> 00:11:47.660
of underscore partition
time, as now defined

00:11:47.660 --> 00:11:49.160
the data that is in the buffer.

00:11:49.160 --> 00:11:52.010
Finally, we come to
automation within BigQuery.

00:11:52.010 --> 00:11:54.725
While we have various ETL
tools such as Cloud Composure

00:11:54.725 --> 00:11:57.380
and Data Fusion to form
various types of ETL,

00:11:57.380 --> 00:11:59.780
sometimes it may be more
efficient to use BigQuery

00:11:59.780 --> 00:12:02.150
to run complex ETL operations.

00:12:02.150 --> 00:12:04.130
BigQuery supports
scripting, by which

00:12:04.130 --> 00:12:06.800
you can run a series of
commands in a specified

00:12:06.800 --> 00:12:11.203
sequence with control
logic, such as while and if.

00:12:11.203 --> 00:12:12.620
And if you want
to save these, you

00:12:12.620 --> 00:12:14.720
can save these as
a reusable program

00:12:14.720 --> 00:12:16.970
called a stored procedure.

00:12:16.970 --> 00:12:19.160
It can also be used
for migrating existing

00:12:19.160 --> 00:12:21.660
stored procedures
from legacy databases

00:12:21.660 --> 00:12:24.020
to bring them into BigQuery.

00:12:24.020 --> 00:12:27.920
This allows you to perform your
in-data warehouse operations

00:12:27.920 --> 00:12:30.320
quickly, without having
the overhead of having

00:12:30.320 --> 00:12:31.570
to move that data outside.

00:12:34.980 --> 00:12:37.260
With this, let me now
turn it over to Gary

00:12:37.260 --> 00:12:43.127
to tell us how BigQuery best
practices are used at Twitter.

00:12:43.127 --> 00:12:44.460
GARY STEELMAN: Thank you, Jagan.

00:12:44.460 --> 00:12:47.473
My name is Gary, and I'm a
software engineer at Twitter.

00:12:47.473 --> 00:12:49.890
Today I going to talk about
how we use BigQuery at Twitter

00:12:49.890 --> 00:12:52.200
and some of the specific
use cases we have,

00:12:52.200 --> 00:12:53.670
and some of the
performance results

00:12:53.670 --> 00:12:56.100
that we've seen from using
BigQuery's optimization

00:12:56.100 --> 00:12:57.610
features.

00:12:57.610 --> 00:13:00.930
So first, I want to go ahead
and just dive right in and talk

00:13:00.930 --> 00:13:04.300
about how we use
BigQuery at Twitter.

00:13:04.300 --> 00:13:07.030
It's important to understand
the scale at which we

00:13:07.030 --> 00:13:09.950
use BigQuery at Twitter.

00:13:09.950 --> 00:13:13.790
We have hundreds of different
users running queries and jobs

00:13:13.790 --> 00:13:14.290
every month.

00:13:16.800 --> 00:13:19.830
They're running tens of
thousands of jobs every day,

00:13:19.830 --> 00:13:22.230
most of which are queries.

00:13:22.230 --> 00:13:25.830
We store data and run queries
in hundreds of different GCP

00:13:25.830 --> 00:13:27.850
projects, with thousands
of different data sets

00:13:27.850 --> 00:13:30.540
storing hundreds of
petabytes of data.

00:13:30.540 --> 00:13:33.330
As you might imagine,
processing that amount of data

00:13:33.330 --> 00:13:35.640
uses a lot of compute
power, and we're

00:13:35.640 --> 00:13:39.160
using over a million
slot hours a day.

00:13:39.160 --> 00:13:41.540
It's important to note
that at this scale

00:13:41.540 --> 00:13:45.730
at Twitter, that's why we care
about performance so much.

00:13:45.730 --> 00:13:49.180
Every little bit of performance
optimization we can make

00:13:49.180 --> 00:13:51.710
helps quite a lot.

00:13:51.710 --> 00:13:54.940
So now let me talk about some
of the different actual use

00:13:54.940 --> 00:13:58.020
cases we have for
BigQuery at Twitter.

00:13:58.020 --> 00:14:00.530
We can think of these broadly
in two different categories,

00:14:00.530 --> 00:14:03.710
analytics projects
and adoption projects.

00:14:03.710 --> 00:14:07.100
The analytics projects you see
here I'll get into a little bit

00:14:07.100 --> 00:14:08.760
more detail later.

00:14:08.760 --> 00:14:10.910
But the adoption
projects are ones

00:14:10.910 --> 00:14:15.140
that we can think of as
already existing and migrating

00:14:15.140 --> 00:14:18.630
from a previous platform
over to BigQuery.

00:14:18.630 --> 00:14:21.950
So for example, people are
migrating from Google Sheets

00:14:21.950 --> 00:14:23.150
and Google Apps scripts--

00:14:23.150 --> 00:14:26.130
or on premise Vertica
and Solr clusters,

00:14:26.130 --> 00:14:28.850
or even using Google Analytics
and moving their use cases

00:14:28.850 --> 00:14:30.650
to BigQuery.

00:14:30.650 --> 00:14:32.330
Teams who are
adopting BigQuery are

00:14:32.330 --> 00:14:35.450
looking for big
gains in performance

00:14:35.450 --> 00:14:37.190
and also maintainability.

00:14:37.190 --> 00:14:39.710
They're also looking for
that rich integration

00:14:39.710 --> 00:14:41.870
with GCP tooling.

00:14:41.870 --> 00:14:44.960
So now, let me talk a little bit
about those analytics projects

00:14:44.960 --> 00:14:50.210
in more detail and some
of the performance gains

00:14:50.210 --> 00:14:54.620
that we've seen for those
analytics use cases.

00:14:54.620 --> 00:14:56.590
It's important to
frame this discussion

00:14:56.590 --> 00:14:59.740
by understanding how we look at
these optimizations at Twitter.

00:14:59.740 --> 00:15:02.460
We look at them at
three different levels,

00:15:02.460 --> 00:15:05.218
the query level, the table
level, and the project level.

00:15:05.218 --> 00:15:06.760
And a lot of those
different features

00:15:06.760 --> 00:15:08.760
that you saw Jagan
talk about earlier you

00:15:08.760 --> 00:15:12.210
can see here categorized into
the different categories.

00:15:12.210 --> 00:15:15.170
We make heavy use of all
of these optimizations,

00:15:15.170 --> 00:15:18.410
because at our scale at Twitter,
every little bit counts.

00:15:18.410 --> 00:15:22.670
We also use the project
level optimizations

00:15:22.670 --> 00:15:26.590
quite a bit, such as slot
reservations and workload

00:15:26.590 --> 00:15:28.270
isolation.

00:15:28.270 --> 00:15:31.060
We try to separate workloads
into separate projects,

00:15:31.060 --> 00:15:33.857
so we don't end up with
noisy neighbor problems.

00:15:33.857 --> 00:15:35.440
So now that we have
an overview of how

00:15:35.440 --> 00:15:37.900
we think about these
features at Twitter,

00:15:37.900 --> 00:15:39.950
let's talk about how
we actually use them

00:15:39.950 --> 00:15:41.830
in some of these different
types of products.

00:15:44.360 --> 00:15:45.910
The first one I'll
talk about today

00:15:45.910 --> 00:15:47.750
is this marketing product.

00:15:47.750 --> 00:15:50.620
The goal of this product is to
generate an internal dashboard

00:15:50.620 --> 00:15:55.180
based on some filters, where
the results of this report

00:15:55.180 --> 00:15:57.490
will actually be used to
drive marketing insights

00:15:57.490 --> 00:15:59.800
and strategies internally.

00:15:59.800 --> 00:16:02.620
Generating this report requires
running multiple BigQuery

00:16:02.620 --> 00:16:04.450
queries for each of
the different sections

00:16:04.450 --> 00:16:06.110
of the report.

00:16:06.110 --> 00:16:08.750
So here, are some of
the first optimizations

00:16:08.750 --> 00:16:13.130
we made were to partition
the input tables by time

00:16:13.130 --> 00:16:17.060
and to give the project
more query slots to use.

00:16:17.060 --> 00:16:21.800
These two steps, partitioning
the input tables by time

00:16:21.800 --> 00:16:23.900
and giving the project
more query slots,

00:16:23.900 --> 00:16:27.260
are generally our very
first two steps every time.

00:16:27.260 --> 00:16:30.110
The reason is because
these are very low effort

00:16:30.110 --> 00:16:32.750
and very high impact steps.

00:16:32.750 --> 00:16:35.450
We will do this
across most projects.

00:16:35.450 --> 00:16:39.260
Here, we also clustered the
input tables by user IDs,

00:16:39.260 --> 00:16:42.240
because that's what
the report is keyed on.

00:16:42.240 --> 00:16:45.440
And we also scheduled the
projection of necessary columns

00:16:45.440 --> 00:16:49.160
out of the source tables and
into a destination table.

00:16:49.160 --> 00:16:50.690
In that destination
table, we also

00:16:50.690 --> 00:16:52.910
join in a couple of
additional columns,

00:16:52.910 --> 00:16:55.670
just so all of the data
for this particular report

00:16:55.670 --> 00:16:58.370
is available in one
table, ready to go.

00:16:58.370 --> 00:17:00.350
Having all your data
in one table like that

00:17:00.350 --> 00:17:03.930
is actually a huge speed
up just in and of itself.

00:17:03.930 --> 00:17:06.300
As part of this, we
also helped this team

00:17:06.300 --> 00:17:09.180
clean up their queries by making
small tweaks here and there,

00:17:09.180 --> 00:17:13.069
such as removing unnecessary
order by clauses.

00:17:13.069 --> 00:17:14.810
All of these things
together resulted

00:17:14.810 --> 00:17:20.180
in a 33% faster average query
time, and a 45% faster end

00:17:20.180 --> 00:17:23.000
to end report generation
time for this use case.

00:17:25.589 --> 00:17:28.060
The next use case that
I'll talk about today

00:17:28.060 --> 00:17:31.450
is the A/B
experimentation project.

00:17:31.450 --> 00:17:35.470
This team already has
an analytics use case

00:17:35.470 --> 00:17:37.750
running on premise in Vertica.

00:17:37.750 --> 00:17:40.360
So in this case, we're migrating
from an on-premise Vertica

00:17:40.360 --> 00:17:43.630
cluster into BigQuery.

00:17:43.630 --> 00:17:46.200
The goal of this
use case is to fetch

00:17:46.200 --> 00:17:49.650
the results of A/B experiments
on the Twitter product

00:17:49.650 --> 00:17:51.870
and render those in a UI.

00:17:51.870 --> 00:17:53.610
Users of the UI
will actually use

00:17:53.610 --> 00:17:58.320
the insights they gain from the
UI to drive product decisions.

00:17:58.320 --> 00:18:01.020
The interesting thing here
is that the table that

00:18:01.020 --> 00:18:03.900
powers this UI has high
hundreds of terabytes

00:18:03.900 --> 00:18:06.910
of data in one place.

00:18:06.910 --> 00:18:09.070
We also have a very low
latency requirement,

00:18:09.070 --> 00:18:13.750
because this is powering
an internal dashboard.

00:18:13.750 --> 00:18:17.910
So here, we partitioned these
input tables by time again

00:18:17.910 --> 00:18:20.460
and gave it more query
slots to execute.

00:18:20.460 --> 00:18:23.760
Again, we do this for
almost every project.

00:18:23.760 --> 00:18:27.240
We also clustered these
tables by more than one ID.

00:18:27.240 --> 00:18:30.367
Like Jagan mentioned, you can
cluster by more than one field.

00:18:30.367 --> 00:18:32.700
And we wanted to do this in
order of highest cardinality

00:18:32.700 --> 00:18:34.680
first to eliminate the
most amount of data

00:18:34.680 --> 00:18:36.550
in a query as possible.

00:18:36.550 --> 00:18:39.570
We also selected only
the necessary columns out

00:18:39.570 --> 00:18:44.760
of these source tables, whereas
the Vertica query before was

00:18:44.760 --> 00:18:47.110
selecting all of the columns.

00:18:47.110 --> 00:18:49.560
And finally, one of the
more interesting performance

00:18:49.560 --> 00:18:53.820
optimizations we made was
using an array aggregation

00:18:53.820 --> 00:18:56.760
clause in BigQuery, where
in Vertica we had been

00:18:56.760 --> 00:19:00.610
using a row number over clause.

00:19:00.610 --> 00:19:06.210
We use this to identify the
most recent row in a set of rows

00:19:06.210 --> 00:19:08.980
to populate part of this UI.

00:19:08.980 --> 00:19:11.190
All of these things
together ended up

00:19:11.190 --> 00:19:12.810
speeding up the queries by 55%.

00:19:16.010 --> 00:19:19.130
And the final project
that I'll talk about today

00:19:19.130 --> 00:19:21.520
is a Tweet analytics project.

00:19:21.520 --> 00:19:23.530
For this project, we
want to generate a report

00:19:23.530 --> 00:19:25.780
of recent actions on entities.

00:19:25.780 --> 00:19:27.550
The interesting
thing about this one

00:19:27.550 --> 00:19:30.010
is that we're migrating from
an on-premise Solr cluster

00:19:30.010 --> 00:19:33.370
into BigQuery, and
also the BigQuery table

00:19:33.370 --> 00:19:36.160
is populated by
BigQuery streaming.

00:19:36.160 --> 00:19:37.990
The table is also massive.

00:19:37.990 --> 00:19:41.930
It's multiple petabytes
of data in a single table.

00:19:41.930 --> 00:19:46.010
Here too, we partitioned
the input tables by time,

00:19:46.010 --> 00:19:49.040
and we gave the project
many, many more queries slots

00:19:49.040 --> 00:19:51.590
to work with.

00:19:51.590 --> 00:19:54.820
We also scheduled a projection
of the necessary columns

00:19:54.820 --> 00:19:57.220
to generate this report
out of the source tables

00:19:57.220 --> 00:19:59.320
into a destination table again.

00:19:59.320 --> 00:20:02.290
Here too, we also saw a
nice speed up with that.

00:20:02.290 --> 00:20:04.310
The most interesting
thing that we saw here,

00:20:04.310 --> 00:20:08.200
though, is we had to tune
our storage set sizes.

00:20:08.200 --> 00:20:11.450
BigQuery tables are backed by
storage sets, which contain

00:20:11.450 --> 00:20:13.490
files, which contain records.

00:20:13.490 --> 00:20:16.310
Storage sets have a
maximum size limit.

00:20:16.310 --> 00:20:19.250
So when a storage set
fills up with records,

00:20:19.250 --> 00:20:21.950
it actually gets committed
and creates a metadata entry

00:20:21.950 --> 00:20:24.230
with it associated
with the table.

00:20:24.230 --> 00:20:25.790
When a query wants
to run on a table,

00:20:25.790 --> 00:20:27.890
it has to process
all the metadata

00:20:27.890 --> 00:20:30.680
for the different storage sets
to decide which storage sets

00:20:30.680 --> 00:20:33.070
actually need to be accessed.

00:20:33.070 --> 00:20:34.710
If there's a lot
of storage sets,

00:20:34.710 --> 00:20:36.670
there's a lot of
metadata entries.

00:20:36.670 --> 00:20:38.620
And processing a lot
of metadata entries

00:20:38.620 --> 00:20:40.850
can actually slow
down your query.

00:20:40.850 --> 00:20:45.580
So in our use case, we worked
with Google engineering

00:20:45.580 --> 00:20:49.990
to increase the size
of our storage sets.

00:20:49.990 --> 00:20:54.670
Larger sized storage sets means
fewer storage sets overall,

00:20:54.670 --> 00:20:57.710
because each one can
hold more records.

00:20:57.710 --> 00:20:59.810
Because there are
fewer storage sets,

00:20:59.810 --> 00:21:02.623
there is less metadata entries
for a query to have to process,

00:21:02.623 --> 00:21:04.040
which actually
ends up speeding up

00:21:04.040 --> 00:21:05.540
your query a good
amount when you

00:21:05.540 --> 00:21:09.430
have a high volume of
streaming inserts into a table.

00:21:09.430 --> 00:21:11.020
All of these
optimizations together

00:21:11.020 --> 00:21:16.780
resulted in 33% faster
queries for this use case.

00:21:16.780 --> 00:21:19.990
And that's the last use case
I going to talk about today.

00:21:19.990 --> 00:21:23.040
I want to give a huge shout
out to everyone at Twitter,

00:21:23.040 --> 00:21:25.410
cross functionally,
at Google PSO,

00:21:25.410 --> 00:21:27.180
and Google Engineering
for all the help

00:21:27.180 --> 00:21:31.380
that they've given up to today.

00:21:31.380 --> 00:21:35.220
Google has helped us build
additional tools for Twitter

00:21:35.220 --> 00:21:37.290
internal data format.

00:21:37.290 --> 00:21:39.780
They've helped us build
dashboards and administration

00:21:39.780 --> 00:21:41.260
tools.

00:21:41.260 --> 00:21:44.860
They've helped us increase
the DML efficiency by changing

00:21:44.860 --> 00:21:46.500
things in the back end.

00:21:46.500 --> 00:21:49.930
And they've increased limits in
GCP all over the place for us.

00:21:49.930 --> 00:21:52.930
Some of the most notable
ones are for VPCSC

00:21:52.930 --> 00:21:55.390
or for the amount of data
that a particular query

00:21:55.390 --> 00:21:57.430
can process in a single go.

00:21:57.430 --> 00:21:58.990
Without all of these
things together,

00:21:58.990 --> 00:22:00.882
we wouldn't be near
where we are today.

00:22:00.882 --> 00:22:03.340
And we're really looking forward
to continuing our adoption

00:22:03.340 --> 00:22:05.500
and growth with
BigQuery at Twitter.

00:22:05.500 --> 00:22:09.130
So thank you to everyone
who's been involved.

00:22:09.130 --> 00:22:11.450
I'll pass it back
over to Jagan now,

00:22:11.450 --> 00:22:14.818
so he can wrap up the
rest of this presentation.

00:22:14.818 --> 00:22:17.110
JAGAN ATHREYA: I hope you've
